1
00:00:00,352 --> 00:00:02,853
So as you learned, you can save and load

2
00:00:03,013 --> 00:00:05,450
sessions. And what happens technically is

3
00:00:05,562 --> 00:00:08,192
that a copy of the model gets created. And

4
00:00:08,208 --> 00:00:11,302
for that copied model, all of your settings,

5
00:00:11,494 --> 00:00:13,819
your system message, your parameter

6
00:00:13,819 --> 00:00:16,833
settings, and your existing chat history is

7
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:20,552
saved and baked into that model, so to say.

8
00:00:21,594 --> 00:00:25,105
And you can see that model if you run ollama

9
00:00:25,441 --> 00:00:28,231
list, because that lists all the models that

10
00:00:28,231 --> 00:00:30,379
have been downloaded onto your system.

11
00:00:31,999 --> 00:00:35,013
You also have ollama ps, which you can run

12
00:00:35,302 --> 00:00:37,835
to list all currently running models.

13
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:41,475
So here, ollama ps does indeed show me the

14
00:00:41,539 --> 00:00:44,409
Gemma 3 model I started because by default,

15
00:00:44,585 --> 00:00:47,472
Ollama keeps them running for 5 minutes. And

16
00:00:47,632 --> 00:00:50,101
after 5 minutes without action, it will

17
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:52,779
remove them from memory so that they no

18
00:00:52,859 --> 00:00:54,751
longer take up memory space.

19
00:00:55,425 --> 00:00:57,878
But this one, since I just used it, is still

20
00:00:58,054 --> 00:00:58,359
running.

21
00:00:58,952 --> 00:01:01,277
And here you can also see the size it's

22
00:01:01,277 --> 00:01:03,009
occupying in memory and

23
00:01:03,667 --> 00:01:05,864
where it's running, if it's on the GPU or a

24
00:01:05,864 --> 00:01:06,457
CPU.

25
00:01:07,035 --> 00:01:09,392
GPU, of course, is better, as you learned.

26
00:01:10,402 --> 00:01:12,663
But Ollama list shows you all the models you

27
00:01:12,808 --> 00:01:14,796
downloaded onto your system or all the

28
00:01:14,796 --> 00:01:16,592
models that are available here.

29
00:01:17,314 --> 00:01:21,291
And that does include that Gemma 3 quantized

30
00:01:21,323 --> 00:01:24,098
12 billion parameters model I downloaded

31
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:24,675
earlier.

32
00:01:25,401 --> 00:01:28,278
But it also includes this S1 model I

33
00:01:28,407 --> 00:01:32,368
created, this copy I created, which is that

34
00:01:32,368 --> 00:01:35,215
Gemma 3 model because that is the model I

35
00:01:35,215 --> 00:01:35,783
saved,

36
00:01:36,335 --> 00:01:39,223
but it's that model with my system message,

37
00:01:39,418 --> 00:01:42,420
with my parameter settings, and with my chat

38
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:43,426
history baked in.

39
00:01:45,091 --> 00:01:47,646
And therefore, if I knew that I want to use

40
00:01:47,743 --> 00:01:49,683
this model, that I want to continue with

41
00:01:49,683 --> 00:01:51,817
that chat history and use all the settings

42
00:01:51,946 --> 00:01:52,787
as I saved them,

43
00:01:53,356 --> 00:01:54,462
when using Ollama run,

44
00:01:55,136 --> 00:01:58,070
instead of using ollama run Gemma 3 and so

45
00:01:58,086 --> 00:02:01,325
on, I could ollama run S1 since that's the

46
00:02:01,325 --> 00:02:03,682
name I chose for saving it. And that will

47
00:02:03,794 --> 00:02:07,354
now restore that session and run that model

48
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:09,534
with all the settings I did save.

49
00:02:10,528 --> 00:02:12,933
Now, of course, over time, as you work with

50
00:02:12,998 --> 00:02:16,108
Ollama, as you experiment with it, and as

51
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,641
you add more and more models, you might end

52
00:02:18,690 --> 00:02:20,838
up with a long list of models. And of

53
00:02:20,934 --> 00:02:23,645
course, These models are taking up space on

54
00:02:23,742 --> 00:02:24,399
your system.

55
00:02:25,362 --> 00:02:28,747
Now, technically, despite it saying here

56
00:02:29,052 --> 00:02:31,747
that each model has a size of roughly 9

57
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:32,293
gigabytes,

58
00:02:33,047 --> 00:02:36,560
Ollama is smart and it won't just copy

59
00:02:37,138 --> 00:02:40,073
models if you save derivatives that are

60
00:02:40,202 --> 00:02:42,624
based on that model, like that S1 model. So

61
00:02:42,624 --> 00:02:45,993
it will actually not take up 18 gigabytes of

62
00:02:46,378 --> 00:02:47,293
disk space here.

63
00:02:47,822 --> 00:02:50,086
Instead, it will just save the

64
00:02:50,713 --> 00:02:53,089
differences, the additional configuration

65
00:02:53,201 --> 00:02:55,899
you applied to that saved model, and then

66
00:02:56,236 --> 00:02:59,046
use that saved metadata in conjunction with

67
00:02:59,046 --> 00:03:02,065
the original model files when you run the S1

68
00:03:02,177 --> 00:03:02,611
model.

69
00:03:03,253 --> 00:03:06,304
So you can easily create derivatives and

70
00:03:06,368 --> 00:03:09,081
customized versions of models without

71
00:03:09,097 --> 00:03:12,373
quickly cluttering up your disk. That's the

72
00:03:12,389 --> 00:03:14,686
good news. But still, of course, over time,

73
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:17,980
you might end up with a long list of models

74
00:03:18,254 --> 00:03:20,359
where you might simply not need all the

75
00:03:20,359 --> 00:03:21,451
models anymore.

76
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:24,794
That's why Ollama also gives you the rm

77
00:03:24,922 --> 00:03:26,931
command, which you can use to remove a

78
00:03:26,931 --> 00:03:29,743
model. And remove means remove. It also

79
00:03:29,743 --> 00:03:31,109
removes it from the disk.

80
00:03:32,491 --> 00:03:36,379
So therefore here I can run ollama rm s1,

81
00:03:36,636 --> 00:03:38,725
and that will remove that s1 model.

82
00:03:39,945 --> 00:03:42,000
Again, in case of this model, since it was

83
00:03:42,033 --> 00:03:44,344
based on that Gemma 3 model, it didn't

84
00:03:44,425 --> 00:03:46,817
actually take up any significant amount of

85
00:03:46,993 --> 00:03:49,385
disk space, but still, of course, there is

86
00:03:49,498 --> 00:03:51,826
no reason to keep old models around if

87
00:03:51,826 --> 00:03:53,303
you're just not using them anymore.

88
00:03:54,619 --> 00:03:57,654
What you can also do with the Ollama command

89
00:03:57,910 --> 00:04:00,945
when managing your locally downloaded models

90
00:04:01,603 --> 00:04:04,669
is use the show command to learn more about

91
00:04:04,669 --> 00:04:05,151
a model.

92
00:04:05,873 --> 00:04:08,039
For example, here I can use Ollama show

93
00:04:08,183 --> 00:04:08,857
Gemma-3,

94
00:04:09,547 --> 00:04:11,424
and then I'll just repeat that

95
00:04:12,531 --> 00:04:13,285
identifier here

96
00:04:14,135 --> 00:04:15,707
to learn more about this model. And

97
00:04:15,739 --> 00:04:17,279
essentially that's the same kind of

98
00:04:17,279 --> 00:04:20,439
information we got with the /show command

99
00:04:20,696 --> 00:04:21,771
when running that model.

100
00:04:22,541 --> 00:04:24,386
Here you can get it without starting that

101
00:04:24,386 --> 00:04:26,102
model just to learn more about it.

102
00:04:27,049 --> 00:04:29,102
And that can of course be particularly

103
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:31,300
helpful if you don't know

104
00:04:32,006 --> 00:04:34,925
what exactly a model was about anymore.

105
00:04:35,534 --> 00:04:37,716
Because of course here with the Gemma 3

106
00:04:37,716 --> 00:04:39,672
model, it's pretty clear that it's, well,

107
00:04:39,961 --> 00:04:42,495
the Gemma 3 model, the 12 billion parameter

108
00:04:42,511 --> 00:04:43,137
version of it.

109
00:04:43,891 --> 00:04:44,805
But what if

110
00:04:45,639 --> 00:04:47,130
you did run that model

111
00:04:48,301 --> 00:04:49,921
and you then set a system message

112
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:52,119
like, you are a friendly

113
00:04:52,776 --> 00:04:53,514
assistant,

114
00:04:54,556 --> 00:04:55,727
and you then saved this

115
00:04:56,641 --> 00:04:59,432
custom M1, whatever, you can choose any

116
00:04:59,448 --> 00:05:00,507
identifier you want.

117
00:05:01,933 --> 00:05:04,271
And then you quit and you went on vacation.

118
00:05:04,872 --> 00:05:06,479
And after a time you came back and you're

119
00:05:06,495 --> 00:05:08,557
wondering, what's this model about?

120
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:09,978
Well,

121
00:05:10,715 --> 00:05:13,109
for situations like this, you can use Ollama

122
00:05:13,109 --> 00:05:13,420
Show.

123
00:05:14,952 --> 00:05:16,816
And you will learn that this was indeed

124
00:05:16,864 --> 00:05:19,981
based on the Gemma 3 model family, the 12

125
00:05:20,013 --> 00:05:20,913
billion parameter

126
00:05:21,443 --> 00:05:24,062
model flavor specifically. And then you

127
00:05:24,095 --> 00:05:26,380
might remember that this was indeed that

128
00:05:26,686 --> 00:05:29,278
custom version of that Gemma 3 model you

129
00:05:29,278 --> 00:05:30,679
saved that had that system message baked in.

130
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,118
So that's why the Ollama show command can be

131
00:05:35,118 --> 00:05:37,883
useful for inspecting and understanding your

132
00:05:37,931 --> 00:05:40,085
models, especially your custom models.
